1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,300
Ever started a new game, pressed Start,

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:04,860
and found yourself still watching a cutscene
minutes later?

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:09,620
Your hands are ready but there's nothing to
do, and that time feels longer than it is.

4
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:14,320
Players don't judge fun by hearing it
explained. They try it and decide.

5
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:18,100
That's why how quickly a game lets people play
in the first few minutes matters.

6
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:21,680
Super Mario Odyssey handles this very quickly.
After a short opening, Mario lands

7
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:27,940
in the Cap Kingdom, and from there the running
and jumping are in the player's hands.

8
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:34,240
The first prompts after the opening are simple
too: look around, run, jump. That is all.

9
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:40,460
Today, let's take apart how Odyssey's first
few minutes are ordered, one system at a time.

10
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,080
The first thing you get after taking control
is the cap throw.

11
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:47,940
Throw it and coins pop and crates break;

12
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:53,620
throw it at an enemy and you become that
enemy. One button carries many uses.

13
00:00:53,880 --> 00:01:00,160
So after a few seconds you can tell what this
game is about: throw, break, take over.

14
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:03,560
Breaking crates and pulling back a Chain Chomp
to launch it both

15
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:07,120
start from the same cap. Instead of memorizing
new buttons,

16
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:09,980
you discover new uses for an action you
already know.

17
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:13,700
When the core action is gathered into one,
there's only one thing to learn at first.

18
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:17,580
The rest grows naturally as you use that
action in more places.

19
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:19,860
So what about the story? Odyssey's opening
starts

20
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:23,980
on an airship where a fight is already
underway.

21
00:01:24,280 --> 00:01:29,260
It doesn't spend long on why Bowser wants a
wedding or why the two are fighting.

22
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:34,040
That part is a short cutscene, not gameplay,
but it drops you straight into the action.

23
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:36,140
Instead of stacking backstory up front,

24
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:39,820
it shows only what's happening and moves
straight on to play.

25
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:43,860
It's fine if the player doesn't know what's
going on yet.

26
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:47,380
Once it's in their hands, the urge to find out
actually carries the story forward.

27
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:52,880
Keep the story, but pull the controls forward.
That's the order of Odyssey's first scene.

28
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,360
Why put play this far forward?

29
00:01:56,740 --> 00:02:00,280
Films and animation can show great visuals and
stories too. But a character moving

30
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:06,080
when you press a button, and a world reacting
to you, is something only games can offer.

31
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,580
In the Cascade Kingdom, throw your cap at a
dinosaur and now you are the dinosaur,

32
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:15,080
smashing through whatever blocked the way.
That's a feel you can't get just by watching.

33
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:18,580
The moment you become the dinosaur, your size,

34
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:20,760
your movement and what you can break all
change at once.

35
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:25,300
Hearing that explained and steering it
yourself are completely different experiences.

36
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:27,680
Let players taste that feel in the first few
minutes,

37
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:31,680
and they'll figure out for themselves what
they'll be doing in this game.

38
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:36,020
The tutorial works the same way. The Cap
Kingdom doesn't set aside practice

39
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:40,660
for cap throws and captures; it lets you use
them right on the main path.

40
00:02:40,940 --> 00:02:43,380
Throw your cap at a frog and you can jump
high, and that height opens the next path.

41
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:47,960
The moment you learn is already the real game.

42
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:50,860
Here's a fun detail: when you start a new
game, the Super Mario Odyssey title

43
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:57,720
sequence only appears after you beat the first
boss. Play comes before the title.

44
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,560
A short opening, a core action gathered into
one cap, a tutorial inside the main game,

45
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:06,700
a title that follows play. That order makes
Odyssey's first few minutes.

46
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:12,420
Try timing your own game's first three
minutes. After pressing Start,

47
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,500
when does the player first move, and does that
movement tell them why the game is fun?

48
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:18,040
Don't make them wait. Let them play first.
